        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the position of the smallest value: which joint is slowest, which sensor reads lowest, which of several candidates is closest (the argmin of their distances).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.argmin <dim>\nWithout a dim, the position counts through the whole tensor as if it were flattened.\nWith one, a dim box appears, and each slice gets its own position.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim searched along: 1 finds the smallest in each row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmin index:\nThe position(s), counting from 0. If the smallest value appears more than once, the first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: in 5 3 8 1 9 the smallest, 1, is at position 3. Bottom: with dim 1, each row's smallest: 3 in the first row (position 1), 0 in the second (position 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.argmax finds the largest. t.min gives the smallest value itself (and, with a dim, its position too). t.topk with largest off finds the several smallest. np.argmin is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the position of the smallest value: which joint is slowest, which sensor reads lowest, which of several candidates is closest (the argmin of their distances).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.argmin <dim>\nWithout a dim, the position counts through the whole tensor as if it were flattened.\nWith one, a dim box appears, and each slice gets its own position.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim searched along: 1 finds the smallest in each row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmin index:\nThe position(s), counting from 0. If the smallest value appears more than once, the first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: in 5 3 8 1 9 the smallest, 1, is at position 3. Bottom: with dim 1, each row's smallest: 3 in the first row (position 1), 0 in the second (position 0).\n\nRELATED:\nt.argmax finds the largest. t.min gives the smallest value itself (and, with a dim, its position too). t.topk with largest off finds the several smallest. np.argmin is the numpy version."
        },
